| Feature               | Abstraction                                                                       | Abstract Class                                                                          | Encapsulation                                                                                           |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Definition**        | The process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features. | A special class declared using the `abstract` keyword that helps implement abstraction. | The process of wrapping data and methods into a single unit and protecting data using access modifiers. |
| **Purpose**           | To hide complexity.                                                               | To provide partial implementation and force subclasses to implement required methods.   | To secure data and control access to it.                                                                |
| **Keyword**           | No keyword (it's a concept).                                                      | `abstract`                                                                              | No keyword (uses `private`, `protected`, `public`).                                                     |
| **Focus**             | What an object does.                                                              | Provides a blueprint for subclasses.                                                    | How data is protected.                                                                                  |
| **Object Creation**   | Not applicable.                                                                   | Cannot create an object directly.                                                       | Objects can be created normally.                                                                        |
| **Methods**           | Achieved using abstract classes or interfaces.                                    | Can contain abstract methods and concrete methods.                                      | Usually contains getters and setters to access private data.                                            |
| **Real-life Example** | You drive a car using the steering wheel without knowing how the engine works.    | A "Shape" class defines `draw()`, while `Circle` and `Rectangle` implement it.          | An ATM hides the bank balance and allows access only through deposit/withdraw operations.               |

